  "recommendation": {
    "word": "hold",
    "why": "Follow-up pain-dark-followups. Post-flip look: the trajectory pain-dark cell is E in both scroll directions on Chromium 156 and Linux WebKit 27.2 at 390 and 640 (run 39852164349, layer engaged on both engines, flip at the same 4 px step in both arms); Safari-on-Mac stays with gpu-safari-lane and the flip-frame lag is not resolved at 4 px steps. toggle_free and ride_exceeds are model assumptions (Chromium refutes toggle_free for style: 4 to 5 recalcs, Lean chromium_style_gt). Backing: the one backing-free arrangement tried, pain:dark-static (no animated layer), has no extra backing (+0 MB, same layers as base) and keeps the Chromium toggle-frame saving (3,413,963 to 3,013,203 px, raster tasks 26 to 20, style recalcs 4 to 3) but loses the WebKit paint saving (band 1500 to 3000 paints 26.5 and 25.2 M px squared against 26.9 and 24.8 M for base and 10.1 and 9.9 M for pain:dark-scroll; 53 and 50 full-stage repaints against 54 and 49 and 5 and 5), and it changes the look: the premise Hidden fails at 390 and 640 (Pain top 370 and 843 px below the flip offset 873, 891.5 needed (Lean hidden_fails_390, hidden_fails_640)), so it is a look-change proposal, not a variant of the same look. The WebKit saving and the 12 MB backing appear together and disappear together in these three arms (INFERRED: both follow from the stage content being split off the sticky backing by the overlap promotion; why the base repaints the whole stage every step is not identified). pain:dark-scroll stays hold; breakpoint gate on pain:dark-static passes on both engines (run 39852250962)."
  }
